noverify: an opening; the question's answer (a right angle, because the q1 component has been removed) is not stated and nothing is computed
@@ reply
**Why this matters**

Gram-Schmidt turns any independent set of vectors into an orthonormal set spanning the same space. That set is the $Q$ of a QR factorisation, the standard route to least-squares fits and a building block of eigenvalue algorithms. You have the first step, normalising $a_1$ into $q_1$, from two days ago; the subtraction step is where it slipped, so start there.

<ask type="short">Take $a_2$ and subtract its component along $q_1$, leaving $v_2 = a_2 - (q_1 \cdot a_2)\,q_1$. What is the angle between $v_2$ and $q_1$, and what makes it come out that way?</ask>

@@@@ open-prior-spectral-theorem-inverse-of-q
situation: open-prior
topic: Symmetric matrices and the spectral theorem
voice: plain
level: hard
before: {"prior":[{"topic":"Orthogonal matrices","daysAgo":4,"finished":true,"plan":["Orthonormal columns","Lengths and angles are preserved","Rotations and reflections"],"right":4,"partly":0,"wrong":1,"missed":["What is the inverse of an orthogonal matrix Q?"]}]}
expect: {"askMustNot":["transpose","Q\\^T","Q\\^\\{T\\}","\\\\top"],"must":["orthogonal matrices","Q\\\\Lambda Q\\^\\{-1\\}"],"mustNot":["Q\\^T","transpose"]}
noverify: an opening; it states the theorem and asks for the inverse of Q without giving it; nothing is computed
@@ reply
**Why this matters**

A real symmetric matrix has real eigenvalues and a full set of eigenvectors that can be chosen perpendicular, so it can be written $A = Q\Lambda Q^{-1}$ with $Q$ orthogonal and $\Lambda$ diagonal. Covariance matrices, Hessians of loss functions and graph Laplacians are all symmetric, which is why PCA, second-order optimisation and spectral clustering work the way they do. This rests on orthogonal matrices from four days ago: the factorisation is cheap to use only because inverting $Q$ costs nothing, and that was the one question missed there.

<ask type="short">$Q$ is a square matrix with orthonormal columns. How do you get $Q^{-1}$ from $Q$ without any elimination, and which product would you compute to confirm it?</ask>

